From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 19:20:31 -0400
Subject: spaces: one tab strip, filtered by the active Space

Spaces, complete. One SpaceModel per normal window (ADR 0015: every tab stays
in the one TabStripModel and carries a Space id; switching changes only which
tabs are shown). The model observes the strip, so a tab is filed under the
active Space the moment it opens, or under the Space it was restored from.
Tab traversal and next-tab-after-close consult one hidden-tab predicate on
the strip, so no keystroke lands on a tab from another Space. Switching
settles the active tab before anything hides: the tab last active there,
else the first in the Space, else an essentials tab, else a new tab opened
in the Space. Closing the last visible tab of a Space opens a replacement.
Deleting a Space moves its tabs to the active one.

The sidebar: a switcher of 28 DIP chips spread across the bottom with a +
at the end; a name pill floating above the hovered chip with no layout
shift; a context menu for icon, colour, rename-in-place, "move current tab
here" and delete; the window tinted from the active Space once there is more
than one; Chromium-pinned tabs as the essentials row above every Space; a
second per-Space pin tier above the Clear line; Clear on the separator
between pinned and unpinned tabs, closing the Space's unpinned tabs; a tab
dragged onto a chip moves to that Space (a drop handler that takes ownership
of a tab is unit-tested with a fake controller, which is why it is on).

Persistence rides the session's per-window and per-tab extra data: the
Space list and active id per window, membership and per-Space pin per tab,
written on insert so nothing depends on when a menu was opened. Dev
parameters (extra_spaces, pin_tabs) recreate multi-Space states for captures
and tests because a headless harness cannot pin or switch.

docs/features/spaces.md is the definition of done (B1-B14); the window-level
tests live in space_model_window_unittest.cc.

Persistence has to survive Chromium's own session-log rebuild. The session
service rebuilds the log from the live browser at startup and every 250
writes, and knows nothing about extra data features wrote; a window with
three Spaces came back with one on the second restart. A small provider
registry in chrome/browser/sessions (stedding_session_rebuild.h) lets a
feature re-emit its per-window and per-tab extra data during the rebuild,
and the model also writes its Space list once the window is tracked (on the
first tab insert), because a run in which the user never touches Spaces
would otherwise write a log with no Spaces in it. Startup continues where
it left off by default, since the arrangement lives in the session. The
restored active Space's tint shows from the first frame. Every surface names
an unnamed Space the same way ("Space N").

A Space the user has not given an icon still shows a glyph, taken from the
same list the context menu offers, so chips are never blank discs.

The sidebar's "+ New Tab" row sits under the Clear line above the unpinned
tabs and opens what Cmd+T opens; the bottom "+" pill is hidden, its container
kept as the flexible spacer that holds the Space switcher at the bottom.

The Space context menu names each icon (Home, Work, ...) and shows a
swatch beside each colour.

Why: Spaces are the feature people stay in Arc for, and the workflow layer
the sidebar exists to serve; a switcher that changes an id without moving
the active tab is a picture of a feature.
Removable when: the tab collection tree grows a first-class notion of tab
sets and Spaces become a collection type (ADR 0015, option C), and the
session service can express tab sets itself. Never short of that.
